<div style="border: 1px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; background-color: #eaf2f8; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Discovering Our Classroom – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-style: italic; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;" colspan="2"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Our Classroom</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Book Is on the Table.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Share simple stories from books or personal creations. Interactive: Interact with questions and answers about a classroom topic.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can share simple stories about object locations (e.g., "Maria is looking for the book. Where's the book? Oh, it's on the desk."). Can ask and answer questions about people in the classroom (e.g., "What is the teacher's name?" "The teacher's name is Miss Maria."). Can describe classroom objects using basic words and phrases (e.g., "I have a red pencil.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game using classroom objects and prepositions. For example: "Simon says, put your <b>book</b> /b/ <b>on</b> /ɔ/ /-n/ the <b>table</b> /t/." "Simon says, hold your <b>pencil</b>." Students follow commands, activating prior knowledge of classroom vocabulary and prepositions. The teacher brings realia (a book, a pencil, a marker, an eraser).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a book and a desk. Teacher explicitly models: "Where is the <b>book</b>? It's <b>on</b> the <b>desk</b>." Teacher points to the object and location. Teacher exaggerates the sounds /b/ in "book" and /t/ in "table/desk" and the blending of /ɔ/ /-n/ in "on" for phonemic awareness. Then, the teacher models asking, "Where is the pencil?" and answering, "It's in the pencil case."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a book on a chair: "Is the book under the chair?" (Expected answer: "No, it's on the chair.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the word "on" on the board: "Can you say /ɔ/ /-n/ 'on'?" (Checking pronunciation of the preposition).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Discovering Our Classroom".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated short dialogue displayed on the board or projector, or an audio dialogue with accompanying visuals. Genre: Classroom Conversation. Register: Informal. Format: Interactive dialogue between two students looking for school supplies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Maria: "Oh no! Where is my <b>book</b>?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Carlos: "Look! Is it <b>on</b> the <b>desk</b>?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Maria: "No, it's not here. Is it <b>next to</b> the <b>marker</b>?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Carlos: "Hmm... Oh! It's <b>behind</b> the <b>notebook</b>, on the <b>shelf</b>!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Maria: "Yay! Thank you, Carlos!"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Locating objects and places: Using appropriate prepositions to describe familiar object places (e.g., "The book is on the table.") Confirming object locations in the classroom: "It's here." "It's on the shelf."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays/reads the dialogue. Students listen and point to the objects mentioned on a classroom poster.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks gist questions: "Who is looking for a <b>book</b>?" "Is the <b>book</b> <b>on</b> the <b>desk</b> at first?" "Where does Carlos find the <b>book</b>?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> WH-questions ("Where is...?"), Prepositions of place ("in, on, next to, between, behind"), Present simple ("The book is..."), Vocabulary (desk, book, marker, board, shelf, eraser, paper, chair, crayon, notebook, ruler, pencil, pencil case, pen, map). Pronunciation of /b/, /t/, /ɔ/ /-n/ (on), /ˈp-eɪp-ər/ (paper).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Listen and Repeat the Location!"</b> The teacher holds up various classroom objects (book, crayon, ruler) and places them in different locations. Teacher points and says: "The <b>book</b> is <b>on</b> the <b>desk</b>." Students repeat chorally and individually, focusing on clear pronunciation of /b/, /t/, and /ɔ/ /-n/. "The <b>crayon</b> is <b>in</b> the <b>pencil case</b>." "The <b>ruler</b> is <b>next to</b> the <b>notebook</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Sentence Builder Chain."</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by placing an object (e.g., a pen) somewhere and saying, "The <b>pen</b> is <b>on</b> the <b>chair</b>." The first student places another object (e.g., an eraser) and says, "The <b>eraser</b> is <b>next to</b> the <b>pen</b>." The next student continues the chain, adding another object and describing its location relative to one of the existing objects. Encourage full sentences and correct prepositions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check: "Ask Your Partner!"</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives two classroom flashcards (e.g., "book" and "shelf"). Student A asks, "Where is the <b>book</b>?" Student B points to a visible "shelf" in the classroom or on a poster and answers, "It's <b>on</b> the <b>shelf</b>." Then they swap roles. Teacher circulates, monitoring for correct grammar and pronunciation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Our Classroom".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Classroom Object Scavenger Hunt & Report." Collaborative pair work followed by an oral report to another pair.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides the class into pairs. Each pair receives a short list of 3-4 classroom objects to "find" (e.g., "Find: a red <b>pencil</b>, a blue <b>marker</b>, Miss Maria's <b>notebook</b>, the class <b>map</b>"). The objects should be real and placed in various locations around the classroom before the activity.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs walk around the classroom to locate the objects on their list. As they find each item, they verbally confirm its location to their partner using the target language: "Where is the red <b>pencil</b>?" "It's <b>in</b> the <b>pencil case</b> <b>on</b> the <b>desk</b>." They practice using "Excuse me," when navigating around other students.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After all items are found, pairs team up with another pair (forming a group of four).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Within the group of four, each original pair takes turns presenting their findings to the other pair. For example, "We found the class <b>map</b>. It's <b>next to</b> the <b>board</b>." The listening pair can ask questions like, "Is the <b>marker</b> <b>on</b> the <b>shelf</b>?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages fluency, collaborative interaction, and the use of "Excuse me," for respectful classroom interaction.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Classroom Object Scavenger Hunt & Report." The teacher listens to pairs/groups as they interact and present.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will participate in the "Classroom Object Scavenger Hunt & Report," orally asking and answering questions about object locations and reporting their findings to peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of target sounds /b/, /t/, and blending of /ɔ/ /-n/ (on), /ˈp-eɪp-ər/ (paper).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Correctly uses nouns (book, desk, marker, paper, etc.) and prepositions (in, on, next to, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Accuracy:</b> Forms clear WH-questions ("Where is...?") and provides accurate answers ("It's on/in/next to the...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and Interaction:</b> Participates actively with peers and uses polite phrases like "Excuse me," when appropriate.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick self-assessment before leaving the classroom.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can ask "Where is the...?" (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can say "It's on/in/next to the..." for classroom objects. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy for you today when talking about classroom objects?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult for you today, and what do you want to practice more?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students confidently use WH-questions and prepositions of place in their interactions?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the "Classroom Object Scavenger Hunt & Report" engaging and effective for speaking practice? How could it be improved?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific vocabulary or grammar points need more reinforcement? Were pronunciation challenges common (e.g., /b/ vs /t/ or blending "on")?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support writing? (e.g., more structured sentence frames, additional visual aids for prepositions, pairing stronger with weaker students).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job speaking about our classroom today! Tomorrow, we will use these words and sentences to write short descriptions about our classroom and what we like about it (Lesson 4: Writing Composition)."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>